A||||5||White_88||Engine .died.. What Next .. V8 or V6
Z||000000||White_88||07-04-99||05:04 PM||||I have a 4 cylinder and with 153,000 on it, it just finally expired gracefully.  The question now is, What do I do.  I have several Options:  I can either do the V8 route a la V8Archie, or Gary Zumalt. or I can put a V6 engine from another Fiero.<p>My Questions are?  If I choose to go the V8 route .. which is better, the small block chevy route of the Cadillac Northstar route.  Are there any known problems with the added weight?<p>If I choose to put in a V6 from a wrecked Fiero, is the exchange straight forward?  Has anybody turned a 4 into a 6 before?  I am sure I can find a good V6 engine from V8 archie that he has pulled from a Fiero to put a V8 into.<p>Any inputs would be greatly appreciated.
Z||000001||Eric||07-04-99||05:29 PM||||The V6 would be a whole lot more economical.  However, if price is no issue, the Northstar is the best option if you don't mind driving an automatic.<p>The springs would have to be changed if you put in a V8 or V6.  I think a Formula or GT would make a good donor for the springs and rear sway bar.<p>You could put a 2.8, 3.1, or 3.4 motor in your car with no real modifications.  If it was me in your position, I would go with a rebuilt V6, but if you got the dough, the Northstar would be a screamer.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||07-04-99||06:37 PM||||White88, I am putting a V6 into an 88 coupe right now. look for the thead "HELP please" 
Z||000003||batboy||07-05-99||09:50 AM||||I sat down and came up with 10 engines swap combinations for the Fiero. Each has it's advantages and disadvantages. Most of the swaps require expensive conversion kits. Almost all of these will be more work and money than you think it will be at first.<p>1) Rebuild the 2.5 engine or replace it with another 2.5. This option is the cheapest and easiest route. The 2.5 is reliable and efficient, but lacks in power. They're rare, but if you could find a 2.5 Super Duty engine, they are a beefy high output motor.<p>2) Quad-4 (a 16 valve) engine (185 hp) is a light weight 4 cylinder that can be swapped in with a kit. Engines are expensive to work on especially the cams and valve train.<p>3) The 2.8 V6, find a parts Fiero with the right engine and swap all the necessary stuff over, including the engine compartment wiring harness. This swap is time consuming but fairly straight forward since the 2.8 was one of the factory engines offered in the Fiero.<p>4) The 3.1 V6 is available with cast iron heads like found on early 1990's GM front wheel drive minivans or with high output aluminum heads. A little more power than the 2.8 and they will bolt right up to the transmission and cradle.<p>5) The 3.4 V6 is a rear wheel drive engine, so the starter must be moved to the other side of the engine and several other modifications done to allow it to bolt in.<p>6) The 3.4 Twin Dual Cam V6 (215 hp, 24 valves) can be swapped in using an aftermarket kit. Engines are expensive to work on especially the cams and valve train.<p>7) The 3800 V6 can be swapped in with a conversion kit that runs up to $2,000. This engine is a little expensive to buy and swap in, but it does fit very nice, like it was meant to be in the Fiero (it is a Pontiac engine too). If you use a 1995 or newer engine, they put out 205 hp stock and there is a supercharged version of this engine that produces 250 ponies.<p>8) Chevy 4.3 V6 engine is the 6 cylinder version of the Chevy 350 V8. You must use an aftermarket small block V8 conversion kit, but it fits into the Fiero without cutting into the rear wheel well like with the V8. Conversion kit is not cheap, but there are tons of high performance parts available for the 4.3 liter and they put out a lot more power than the 2.8/3.1 V6's.<p>9) Cadillac Northstar aluminum V8 (275-300 hp) is a very expensive, but nice way to go. The engine is light and compact for a V8 and provides silky smooth power, but I believe you must use the automatic transmission too with this swap.<p>10) Chevy small block V8 (350 is the most common) uses an expensive conversion kit and a fair amount of modification. Small bock engines are cheap and plentiful, more high performance goodies available for it than any other engine. Lots of torque and raw power! This engine is a little big for the Fiero so handling suffers a little.<br>
Z||000004||Racer 1||07-06-99||01:05 PM||||My choice would be a quad 4 HO engine.Simply because its awesome and light.Or get the chevy 3800 supercharged devil thats a hot little engine also.Or get the best engine ever a GEO Metro 3 cyld 1.0 engine this engine is the bomb!
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||07-06-99||02:17 PM||||I wouldn't mind having a 4.3 in a Fiero. My 94 S-10 has the enhanced version(190hp and 253 ft.lbs.of torque) and that thing is very fast for a truck.<p>Hmmmm....There is a nice 88 coupe for sale close by....Maybe it would like a 4.3 :)I love these 88s. I guess since my 86 is leaving soon I will have room for an 88 to replace it  :)
